/* ---------- Company-type versions of the universal scenarios ---------- */
// Each universal scenario (tailored:true) is played by every company type. This gives each type its own title, card blurb and
// step scenes; the decisions, consequences, lessons and law notes stay shared. Placeholders in braces are filled from the scenario's vars.
const TT_VERSIONS = {
  ato:{
    social:{title:"The crypto giveaway hijack", blurb:"Stolen passwords unlock creator accounts with millions of followers, and the first posts are crypto giveaways. Stop the attack, get creators back in, and decide who needs to know.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed logins jumped 40-fold, most from one proxy network cycling through passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 creators say they are locked out or see posts they never made. Where the attackers got in, they are posting crypto giveaway links to millions of followers. Your on-call lead asks whether to pull logins entirely.",
      "About 1,100 creator accounts with large followings were taken over before the block. Attackers changed recovery emails, pinned scam posts, and some followers have already sent money. Creators are flooding support from new emails and backup accounts, demanding access back and the scam posts gone. A few are already telling the story on a rival app.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, direct messages and account history for affected creators, and some had business email and phone on file. Scope is still moving, since reviewers keep finding more hijacked accounts. One executive argues the passwords were real, so this is the creators' problem, not yours.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Big creators are asking the same thing in public, and two have threatened to take their audiences elsewhere. Security wants everyone on two-step login by tomorrow; Product worries that locks out the casual users who make up most of your daily traffic."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The payout switch", blurb:"Leaked passwords open seller accounts, and the attackers' first stop is the payout page. With Thursday's seller payments three days away, stop the switch before the money moves.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed seller logins jumped 40-fold, most from a rented botnet trying passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 sellers say they are locked out or got an email saying their payout details changed. Where attackers get in, they switch the payout bank account to their own before Thursday's payment run. Your on-call lead asks whether to close seller login entirely.",
      "About 1,100 seller accounts were taken over before the block. On most of them the payout bank account now points somewhere new, and a handful of early-payout sellers have already lost money. Sellers are emailing support from whatever address they can, asking for their accounts back and for Thursday's payout to go to the right bank.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, bank details and order history for affected sellers, many of whom are sole traders using home addresses. Reviewers are still finding accounts that were touched but not changed. Your head of seller operations argues the passwords were real, so this is not your breach.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Your biggest sellers want a promise before the next payout run, and the finance team wants anything that ends manual payout reversals. Security has proposed switching on mandatory two-step login for every seller tonight."]},
    fintech:{title:"The emptied wallets", blurb:"Leaked passwords let attackers into customer accounts, and instant transfers empty balances in seconds. Stop the bleeding without shutting the app, then work out who you have to tell.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed logins jumped 40-fold, nearly all from a few hundred residential proxy addresses trying passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 customers say they are locked out or see transfers they never made. Where attackers get in, they add a new payee and send the balance out by instant transfer. Your on-call lead asks whether to switch off login for everyone.",
      "About 1,100 customer accounts were taken over before the block. Instant transfers are final, but some are still pending at receiving banks, and the fraud desk thinks it can recall part of them. The support queue is full of customers who want their money and their accounts back, and a few callers can't pass the security questions because the attacker changed them.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, balances and transaction history for affected customers. The count is still rising, and your payments license carries its own incident-reporting deadline. Your head of comms wants to go public today, numbers or not; your head of risk says the passwords were real, so there is nothing to report.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. The board wants an answer before the quarterly call, and the fraud desk is still recalling transfers by hand. Security has proposed turning on mandatory two-factor login for every customer tonight."]},
    gaming:{title:"The looted inventories", blurb:"Attackers log into player accounts with passwords leaked elsewhere, strip rare items and currency, and sell them on grey-market sites. Shut it down without taking the game offline.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed logins jumped 40-fold, almost all from a block of hosting-provider addresses trying passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 players say they are locked out or logged in to find their inventory empty. The attackers trade rare items and currency to throwaway accounts and list them on grey-market sites within minutes. Your on-call lead asks whether to take login down.",
      "About 1,100 player accounts were taken over before the block. Rare items and currency went to throwaway accounts and on to buyers, and some have already changed hands twice. Your trade logs can roll back most of it, but every rollback pulls items from someone else. Victims are filling the support queue and the forums, asking for their accounts and their gear back.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, partial payment details, purchase history and friend lists for affected players, some of whom are minors. The numbers keep shifting, and a popular streamer is already calling it a hack on air. Your community lead argues the passwords were real, so this is on the players, not you.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. A big content update ships in three weeks, and the studio head does not want launch week to look like this one. Security's proposal is mandatory two-factor login for every player by tomorrow; the live team worries about the login screen on a Saturday night."]},
    dating:{title:"The blackmail that followed", blurb:"Leaked passwords open member profiles, and attackers download private photos and chats to use for blackmail. Lock the attack out, get members back in safely, and decide what you must disclose.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed logins jumped 40-fold, almost all from one proxy network trying passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 members say they are locked out or find chats marked read that they never opened. Where attackers get in, they download private photos and message history, raw material for sextortion. Your on-call lead asks whether to take login offline for everyone.",
      "About 1,100 member profiles were taken over before the block. Attackers downloaded private photos and full chat histories, changed some login emails, and the first blackmail messages have already reached victims from outside the app. Members are writing to support from new addresses, often in a panic, asking for their profiles back and for someone to tell them what the attacker took.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, sexual orientation, private photos and full chat history for affected members, and some have already been blackmailed. The scope is still unclear because many members haven't logged in since. Your head of marketing argues the passwords were real, so this is the members' failure, not a breach.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Members are deleting photos, a few have deleted accounts, and your board wants a plan before the next press cycle. Security proposes making two-step login mandatory for every member overnight; the growth team says sign-ups will fall off a cliff."]},
    genai:{title:"The stolen API keys", blurb:"Leaked passwords open developer accounts, and the attackers walk off with API keys to run spam and abuse at scale on the victims' bills. Stop them without cutting off every developer.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed console logins jumped 40-fold, most from cloud addresses cycling through passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 developers say they are locked out or see API keys and usage they don't recognize. Stolen keys are generating spam and abusive content at scale, and victims' usage bills are climbing. Your on-call lead asks whether to disable console login for everyone.",
      "About 1,100 developer accounts and API keys were taken over before the block. The stolen keys ran millions of requests, producing spam and abusive content under the victims' names and running up their bills. Developers are emailing support, some from personal addresses because the attacker changed the account email, asking for access back, the keys revoked and the charges wiped.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, usage logs and stored prompts for affected developers, including their end users' data. Reviewers are still tracing which keys were used, and two enterprise customers have contracts requiring notice within 24 hours. Your platform lead argues the passwords were real, so this is the developers' problem, not yours.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Abuse from the stolen keys has put you on two spam blocklists, and the biggest enterprise customer wants to see your plan before renewing. Security proposes forcing two-factor login on every developer account tonight; the developer relations team expects a wave of lockouts and angry forum posts."]},
    gig:{title:"The rerouted earnings", blurb:"Leaked passwords open driver accounts, and attackers quietly change where the week's earnings land. Stop them before Friday's payout without pulling every driver off the road.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed driver logins jumped 40-fold, most from data-center addresses trying passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 drivers and couriers say they are locked out or see a bank account on their profile that isn't theirs. Attackers are redirecting earnings before Friday's weekly payout. The morning rush starts in 20 minutes, and your on-call lead asks whether to shut login down.",
      "About 1,100 driver accounts were taken over before the block. On most, the payout bank account now belongs to the attacker, and some drivers who cash out daily have already lost a day's earnings. Drivers are calling the support line from the roadside and emailing from new addresses, asking for their accounts back and their money. Many can't work until they're restored.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see names, contact details, home addresses, license and vehicle details, bank details and trip history, including riders' pickup addresses, for affected drivers and couriers. The count is still moving as more drivers report. Your head of driver operations argues the passwords were real, so this is on the drivers, not you.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Driver groups are organizing online, and a city council member has asked why drivers went unpaid. Security proposes mandatory two-factor login for every driver and courier tonight; operations points out that most drivers log in from a phone mounted on a dashboard, between jobs."]},
    kids:{title:"The stranger in the inbox", blurb:"Leaked parent passwords open family accounts, and strangers are reading children's profiles and messages. Lock them out fast, restore families safely, and decide what schools and parents need to hear.", scenes:[
      "Overnight, failed logins jumped 40-fold, most from a proxy network trying parents' passwords leaked from other sites. By 06:40, 3,000 families say they are locked out or see a new device on their account. Where attackers get in, they open children's profiles, photos and class messages. School starts in two hours, and your on-call lead asks whether to take login down for everyone.",
      "About 1,100 family and child accounts were taken over before the block. Attackers read children's messages, downloaded profile photos, and in a few cases sent messages to classmates from a child's account. Parents are emailing support from any address they can, some copying the school, asking for their accounts back and for every message the stranger sent to be gone.",
      "Legal asks whether this counts as a personal data breach. Attackers could see children's names, school and class, photos, message history and parents' contact details for affected families. Nobody knows yet how many child profiles were opened, and children's data has stricter rules in several markets. Your head of school partnerships argues the passwords were real, so this is the parents' failure, not yours.",
      "Leadership asks what will stop this happening again. Two school districts have paused their contracts until they see a plan, and a parents' group is asking the same question in the press. Security proposes mandatory two-factor login for every family tonight; the product team says many parents share one login with a grandparent or a babysitter."]}
  },
  insider:{
    social:{title:"The DMs nobody reported", blurb:"A support agent says a contractor on your review team has been reading private messages and location history for people who never hit a queue, including the contractor's ex-partner.", scenes:[
      "A support agent messages you privately. A contractor on the review team has been pulling private messages and location history for accounts that were never in any queue. The agent saw a map of check-ins on the contractor's screen and recognized the name: it was the contractor's ex-partner. The contractor is on shift right now.",
      "Security has pulled the access logs overnight. The contractor looked up 214 users over six months, including the ex-partner, whose location history was opened 40 times. Nothing was exported or forwarded from your systems, but the tool shows full message threads on screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. The ex-partner once reported the contractor for harassment on your platform.",
      "You walk through the review tool with Engineering. Any agent can type any name into the search bar and open that user's inbox and location history, whether or not the account is in a queue. Access logs exist, but nobody reads them; this one surfaced only because a colleague spoke up. Most of your review workforce is contractors.",
      "The contractor was employed by your moderation vendor, not by you. Procurement finds the contract: it covers price, volume and accuracy targets, but says nothing about background checks, access controls or your right to audit. The vendor's account manager emails to say they have 'handled it internally'."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The neighbor's order history", blurb:"A contractor reviewing listings has been pulling buyers' home addresses and order history for people who were never in a queue. One of them lives on the contractor's street.", scenes:[
      "A support agent flags a colleague on the listing-review team. The contractor has been opening buyer records, with home addresses and full order history, for accounts that never appeared in any queue. The agent noticed because the contractor joked about what a neighbor had been ordering, naming the street. The contractor's shift ends at 18:00.",
      "The access logs are in. Over six months the contractor looked up 214 buyers and sellers, including the neighbor, whose address and recent orders were opened a dozen times. Nothing was downloaded or emailed from your systems, but the record view shows everything on one screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. Several lookups cluster around the same few streets.",
      "You sit down with the tools team. The review console lets any agent search any buyer or seller by name and open their address, phone and every order, whether or not a case is open. Access logs are kept for a year, but nobody looks at them. Contractors handle most of your listing and dispute queues.",
      "The contractor was placed by the moderation vendor that runs your listing and dispute reviews. Legal reads the contract: it sets turnaround times and accuracy targets but gives you no right to audit the vendor, no security requirements, and no duty to tell you about incidents. The vendor says the contractor has been let go."]},
    fintech:{title:"The ex-partner's balance", blurb:"A contractor on your fraud-review team has been pulling transaction histories and balances for customers who were never flagged. One of those customers is the contractor's ex-partner.", scenes:[
      "A support agent reports a contractor on the fraud-review team. The contractor has been opening transaction histories and balances for customers who were never flagged for review. The agent overheard the contractor describe where an ex-partner had been spending money last weekend, down to the restaurant. The contractor has a full shift of alerts to work this afternoon.",
      "Security has the logs. Over six months the contractor viewed 214 customers, including the ex-partner, whose account was opened almost weekly. No statements were exported and no transfers were made, but balances and merchant names show on screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. The ex-partner moved to a new city three months ago.",
      "You review the case tool with Engineering. Any agent can search any customer by name and open their balance, transactions and linked cards, with or without an alert on the account. Access logs go to a storage bucket that nobody reads. Your fraud and disputes queues are staffed mostly by contractors, and the regulator expects you to explain how this happened.",
      "The contractor worked for the outsourcing vendor that staffs your fraud and disputes queues. The contract has no audit rights, no security requirements and no duty to report incidents to you, even though your regulator treats outsourced work as your responsibility. The vendor's director offers a written assurance that it won't happen again."]},
    gaming:{title:"The streamer's real name", blurb:"A contractor on your player-reports team has been pulling real names, emails and voice chat recordings for players nobody reported. One of them is a streamer with millions of followers.", scenes:[
      "A support agent sends you a worried message. A contractor on the player-reports team has been opening real names, email addresses and voice chat recordings for accounts that were never reported. The agent saw a well-known streamer's account on the contractor's screen, with the voice clips queued up, and the contractor laughed it off. The streamer's real identity has never been public.",
      "The logs are back. Over six months the contractor looked up 214 players, including the streamer, whose real name, email and 30 voice recordings were opened. Nothing was downloaded from your systems, but the recordings play in the browser and the profile fits on one screen, so screenshots and phone recordings can't be ruled out. The streamer has been swatted before.",
      "Engineering walks you through the player-reports tool. Any agent can search any player by handle or real name and open their email, payment name and every stored voice clip, whether or not a report exists. Access logging is on, but nobody has ever queried it. Contractors work most of the reports queue, especially on launch weekends.",
      "The contractor was supplied by the moderation vendor that handles your player reports overnight. The contract covers response times and cost per ticket, but has no audit rights, no security standards and no duty to report incidents. The vendor's account lead says the contractor was 'a bad apple' and asks what else you need."]},
    dating:{title:"The ex-partner's matches", blurb:"A contractor on your profile-review team has been opening private messages, photos and orientation details for members who were never reported. One of them is the contractor's ex-partner.", scenes:[
      "A support agent asks for a private word. A contractor on the profile-review team has been opening members' private messages, photos and sexual orientation for accounts that were never reported or flagged. The agent saw the contractor scrolling through an ex-partner's conversations with new matches, and heard the contractor joke that the ex-partner's family has no idea who they're dating.",
      "Security has pulled the logs. Over six months the contractor opened 214 members' profiles, including the ex-partner's, whose messages and private photos were viewed more than 50 times. No files were exported from your systems, but photos and chats display in full, so screenshots can't be ruled out. Several of the 214 members are in countries where being outed is dangerous.",
      "You open the review tool with the product lead. Any agent can search any member by name or phone number and see their photos, messages and orientation, whether or not a report exists. Access logs are kept, but nobody reviews them; the only check is a yearly data-protection training. Contractors handle most profile and photo reviews.",
      "The contractor came from the moderation vendor that reviews your profiles and photos around the clock. The contract has no audit rights, no security requirements and no obligation to report incidents to you, and a data protection authority has already asked how you oversee it. The vendor promises it won't happen again."]},
    genai:{title:"The politician's chat history", blurb:"A contractor on your conversation-review team has been reading users' private chats with the assistant, including conversations nobody flagged. One of those users is a local politician.", scenes:[
      "A support agent reports a contractor on the conversation-review team. The contractor has been opening users' private conversations with the assistant for accounts that were never flagged by a classifier or a user report. The agent saw a search for a local council member's name and the contractor reading that person's chats about a health condition. The contractor is still logged in.",
      "The logs are in. Over six months the contractor read conversations from 214 users, including the politician, whose chats about medication and a family dispute were opened repeatedly. Nothing was exported or copied out of your systems, but full transcripts display on screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. An election in the politician's district is six weeks away.",
      "You go through the review tool with the safety engineering lead. Any agent can search any user by name or email and read their full conversation history, whether or not a conversation was flagged. Access logs exist but are never reviewed; the tool was built for a team of five and now serves hundreds. Most of those reviewers are contractors.",
      "The contractor worked for the moderation vendor that labels and reviews flagged conversations for you. The contract covers labeling accuracy and hourly rates, but gives you no audit rights, sets no security standards and requires no incident reporting. The vendor's CEO sends a one-line note: 'Dealt with. Won't recur.'"]},
    gig:{title:"The reviewer who knew the route", blurb:"A contractor on your safety-review team has been pulling customers' home addresses and trip histories for people who never filed a complaint. One of them is the contractor's ex-partner.", scenes:[
      "A support agent reports a contractor on the safety-review team. The contractor has been opening customers' home addresses and full trip histories for accounts with no complaint, no incident and no open case. The agent noticed the same name searched every few days and recognized it as the contractor's ex-partner from a conversation last month. The contractor is working tonight's shift.",
      "The logs come back from Security. Over six months the contractor looked up 214 customers, including the ex-partner, whose new home address and nightly drop-off times were viewed 60 times. No data was exported from your systems, but the trip map and address show on one screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. The ex-partner moved without telling the contractor.",
      "You sit with the tools team. The safety console lets any agent search any customer or driver by name and open their home address, saved places and every trip, whether or not a case is open. Access logs exist, but nobody reviews them. Contractors handle most of your safety and support queues, including the overnight shift.",
      "The contractor was staffed by the moderation vendor that runs your overnight safety queue. The contract sets staffing levels and response times but has no audit rights, no security requirements and no duty to report incidents. The vendor says it has retrained the team and considers the matter closed."]},
    kids:{title:"The child next door", blurb:"A contractor on your classroom-chat review team has been looking up children's names, schools and messages for accounts nobody reported. One of the children lives on the contractor's street.", scenes:[
      "A support agent reports a contractor on the classroom-chat review team. The contractor has been opening children's full names, schools and private messages for accounts that were never reported or flagged. The agent saw the contractor search a child's name and remark that the child lives a few doors down. The contractor is scheduled through Friday.",
      "Security has the logs. Over six months the contractor looked up 214 children and parents, including the neighbor's child, whose school, class schedule and messages were opened again and again. Nothing was exported from your systems, but the record shows everything on one screen, so screenshots can't be ruled out. Most of the 214 accounts belong to children under 12.",
      "You walk through the review tool with Engineering. Any agent can search any child by name and open their school, class roster and message history, whether or not a report exists. Access logs are kept for compliance, but nobody reads them. Contractors handle most of your chat and content reviews, and schools are already asking questions.",
      "The contractor worked for the moderation vendor that reviews your classroom chats. The contract has no audit rights, no background-check requirement and no duty to report incidents to you. School districts are asking what you require of vendors, and the vendor's answer is that it has 'full confidence' in its team."]}
  },
  emergency:{
    social:{title:"The post six friends saw", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a teenager tells a handful of friends they will end their life tonight. Then police call asking for data, and they don't have a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday, and a 16-year-old has posted to a friends-only audience that they will end their life tonight. Two friends reported it within minutes, and the weekend reviewer notes that the post technically breaks your self-harm policy. The full safety team is off until Monday, and you only know the person's location to city level.",
      "At 23:40 a police officer calls your emergency line. They've spoken to one of the friends and have the account name, but no address. They ask for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and say there's no time to get a warrant. The account also holds four years of private messages.",
      "It's Sunday morning. The weekend reviewer who handled the case messages you to say they didn't sleep. It's their third serious case this month, and they're due back on the self-harm queue at noon. Legal has offered to 'just take these from now on.'",
      "Two weeks on, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of safety happened to be online at 23:10 on a Saturday. The reviewer had never been trained on it, and police found your emergency line through a web search."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The seller who lost everything", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a seller whose shop was shut down tells support they've lost everything and will end their life tonight. Then police call for their details, without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. A seller whose shop was suspended on Friday writes to support that they've lost everything and plan to end their life tonight. Your weekend agent notes that self-harm statements breach the seller conduct policy, and the escalations team is off until Monday. The registered address is a mailbox, so you only know the person's location to city level.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. The seller's partner has reported them missing and mentioned the shop. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and says a warrant would take until morning. The account holds three years of order records and buyer messages.",
      "Sunday morning, the weekend agent who took the chat asks for a word. They're shaken: it's their third serious case this month, including a buyer threat, and they say they can't face the seller support queue today. Legal has offered to take any future 'crisis chats' off the support team's hands.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of seller support saw the chat alert on their phone at 23:10. The weekend agent didn't know an emergency line existed, and the officer reached it only after calling the main sales number twice."]},
    fintech:{title:"The customer after the scam", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a customer who lost their savings to a scam tells your chat agent they will end their life tonight. Then police call for data, with no warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. A customer who sent their savings to a fake investment firm is back in chat, and they tell your agent they plan to end their life tonight. The weekend script covers fraud, not this; self-harm statements fall under your acceptable-use policy. The address on their ID is stale, so you only know the person's location to city level.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. A family member has reported the customer missing and mentioned the scam. The officer wants the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, including where the card was last used, and says there's no time for a warrant. The account also holds two years of transactions and the full chat history.",
      "Sunday morning, the chat agent who handled it is in early, shaken, re-reading the transcript. It's their third serious case this month, after two other scam victims in distress. Their lead has already put them on the 10:00 chat shift, and legal is asking whether its team should take these cases instead.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of fraud happened to be online and knew the emergency line existed. The weekend chat agents had never heard of it, and the officer reached you through the number on the back of the customer's card."]},
    gaming:{title:"The squadmate's report", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a player tells their squad in voice chat that they'll end their life after the match. A squadmate reports it, and police call for data without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. A squadmate's report arrives with a 30-second voice clip from a match still in progress: a player saying they'll end their life once it's over. The player's record is clean, the clip breaks your self-harm rules as written, and the weekend support shift is two people. You only know the person's location to city level, from their IP.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. The squadmate called the police themselves and gave the gamertag, but nothing else. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and says a warrant would take hours they don't have. The account also holds years of text chat and friend lists.",
      "Sunday morning, the support agent who handled the case tells you they listened to the clip a dozen times and couldn't sleep. It's their third serious case this month, after a swatting threat and a harassment case involving a minor. They're rostered on the player safety queue from 10:00. Legal has offered to take imminent-risk reports from now on.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your community safety lead was playing that night and saw the escalation ping. The two weekend agents had never been trained on imminent risk, and the officer found your emergency line through a forum post."]},
    dating:{title:"The frightened match", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a member tells their match they plan to end their life tonight, and the match hits report. Then police call for the member's data without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. A frightened match has reported a conversation: after a first date fell through, a member wrote that they plan to end their life tonight. Location sharing is off, so you only know the person's location to city level. Your weekend safety agent notes that self-harm messages breach the community guidelines, and the full safety team is back Monday.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. The match called the police, but only knows the member's first name, age and photos. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and says there's no time for a warrant. The account also holds months of private conversations with other matches.",
      "Sunday morning, the safety agent who handled it messages you to say they're shaken. It's their third serious case this month, after a sexual assault report and a stalking case. They're due on the safety queue at 10:00, and legal has suggested its on-call lawyer should take 'anything with police involved' from now on.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of trust and safety happened to be awake and checking the report queue. The weekend agent had no training on imminent risk, and the officer reached you by messaging the company's social media account."]},
    genai:{title:"The chat at 23:10", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a user tells your chatbot they have the means in front of them and plan to end their life tonight. Then police call for data without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. Your safety classifier has flagged a conversation for human review: a user has told the assistant they have the means in front of them and plan to end their life tonight. Flagged chats are normally reviewed on weekdays; self-harm content breaches your usage policy. You only know the person's location to city level, from their IP.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. A relative received a worrying message that mentioned the assistant and called the police, but doesn't know the user's current address. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, with no warrant. The account holds eight months of conversations on every subject from work to health.",
      "Sunday morning, the reviewer who handled the escalation tells you they read the whole conversation, all forty turns, and can't stop thinking about it. It's their third serious case this month, after a child safety flag and another self-harm chat. They're on the flagged-content queue from 10:00, and legal has asked whether its team should own 'anything involving police' instead.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of safety happened to be online and recognized the classifier alert. The weekend reviewer had never seen an imminent-risk case, the escalation path was a chat message to one person, and the officer found your emergency line by asking the assistant itself."]},
    gig:{title:"The deactivated courier", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a courier deactivated by your fraud system messages support to say they plan to end their life tonight. Then police call for their data without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. A courier deactivated by your fraud model that afternoon writes to support that they plan to end their life tonight. They can still use chat; self-harm statements breach the courier conduct policy, and appeals are handled on weekdays. The app stopped tracking them at deactivation, so you only know the person's location to city level.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. The courier's housemate called the police after the courier left on their bike, delivery bag and all, without saying where. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and says there's no time for a warrant. The account holds two years of delivery routes and chat logs.",
      "Sunday morning, the support agent who handled the chat is shaken; they've been with you six weeks. It's their third serious case this month, after a bad crash and an assault on a courier. They're rostered on the courier support queue at 10:00, and legal has offered to take 'anything like this' from now on.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of courier operations was online watching Saturday order volumes and saw the escalation. The support agent had never been trained on imminent risk, and the officer reached your emergency line only after a restaurant gave them a delivery manager's number."]},
    kids:{title:"The class chat after hours", blurb:"Late on a Saturday, a 13-year-old writes in a class chat that they plan to end their life tonight, and classmates reply. Then police call for the child's data without a warrant.", scenes:[
      "It's 23:10 on a Saturday. Your keyword filter flags a class chat message: a 13-year-old has written that they plan to end their life tonight, and classmates are replying. The class teacher is offline, flags normally go to school staff on Monday, and self-harm content breaches the student conduct rules. You have the school's name but only know the person's location to city level.",
      "At 23:40 an officer calls your emergency line. A classmate's parent rang the police, but the school office is closed and nobody can reach the principal. The officer asks for the account's phone number, IP address and recent location data, and says there's no time for a warrant. The account also holds the child's full chat and assignment history.",
      "Sunday morning, the moderator who handled the case messages you to say they're shaken. It's their third serious case this month, after a grooming report and a serious bullying case. They're due on the class chat queue at 10:00, and legal has offered to take 'all the police ones' from now on.",
      "Two weeks later, your review finds the protocol worked, but only because your head of child safety happened to be online and knew which police force covered the school. The weekend moderator had never been trained on imminent risk, and the officer reached you through a parent who had the sales team's number."]}
  },
  bots:{
    social:{title:"The sock puppet surge", blurb:"40,000 new accounts appear overnight and start pushing the same political line. Stop the inflow, find the real people among them, and fix the growth numbers they inflated.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with near-identical handles and the same stock profile photos. A promotion for your new creator fund went live yesterday, so some are real. By morning the accounts are reposting the same three political slogans and flooding replies under news posts. Nobody has decided whether to act or keep watching.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still there, and your team has to work out which are real. Four reviewers are free today. The data team says most of the accounts share a few dozen device fingerprints, signed up in bursts of a few hundred, and post within minutes of each other. About a third came through data-center IP ranges; the rest look residential.",
      "The operator has adapted. New accounts now trickle in at a few hundred an hour through residential proxies, and each one passes your sign-up challenge. Your fraud analyst finds the challenges are being farmed out to paid human solvers. The accounts stay quiet for a day, then boost the same slogans, and a day-old account gets the same reach in feeds as anyone else.",
      "Finance wants a number for the damage: reviewer hours, ad impressions served to fakes, and the creator fund payouts that nearly went out. Marketing wants to know why last week's record sign-up chart was wrong, since they already showed it to the board. The growth team's promotion is the obvious target."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The five-star stampede", blurb:"40,000 fresh buyer and seller accounts appear overnight, and by morning they are posting five-star reviews on counterfeit listings. Stop the inflow without deleting the real shoppers among them.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with similar names and the same handful of shipping addresses. A free-listing promotion for new sellers started Monday, so real people are in the mix. By morning, new seller accounts are listing counterfeit headphones and handbags, and new buyer accounts are leaving five-star reviews on them within minutes of listing.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still on the marketplace, and your team has to work out which are real. The data team sees most of them sharing a few dozen device fingerprints and payment-method hashes, signing up in bursts, and reviewing each other's listings. Roughly a third came in from data-center IP ranges. Your integrity team has five people free this week.",
      "The sellers behind the counterfeits have adapted. New accounts now arrive slowly through residential proxies, a few hundred an hour, and every one passes your sign-up challenge. Your analyst traces the solutions to a paid human solving service. The new accounts wait a day, then list goods and review each other, and the fake listings reach search results by day two.",
      "Finance wants the bill: refunds on counterfeit orders, reviewer hours, and free-listing credits claimed by fakes. Marketing wants to know why the growth team's free-listing promotion showed a record week of new sellers. A brand owner's lawyer has also asked how many counterfeit listings carried five-star reviews."]},
    fintech:{title:"The bonus harvest", blurb:"40,000 new wallets open overnight, each lined up to collect a $50 welcome bonus. Stop the payouts without freezing the real customers who joined the same night.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with similar names and birthdates a few days apart. Your $50 sign-up bonus campaign has been running since Friday, and it pulled in real customers too. By morning the new accounts are making the minimum qualifying deposit, and the first bonuses are queued to pay out at noon.",
      "Your team has to work out which of the 40,000 new accounts belong to real people. Fraud analytics sees the same few hundred devices behind most of them, deposits of exactly the qualifying amount from the same few funding cards, and sign-ups in bursts. About a third came through data-center IP ranges. Compliance reminds you every closure needs a reason on file.",
      "The bonus farmers have adapted. New accounts now arrive at a few hundred an hour through residential proxies, and each passes your sign-up challenge, since the ring pays human solvers a few cents each. The accounts use different funding cards and wait a day before depositing. Your payments lead says the bonus still pays on the first qualifying deposit, no questions asked.",
      "Finance wants to know how much went out the door: bonuses already paid, chargebacks on the funding cards, and the analyst hours. Marketing wants to know why the growth team's campaign report showed its best week ever. The regulator's quarterly return is due, and it asks for active-customer numbers."]},
    gaming:{title:"The overnight gold farm", blurb:"40,000 new accounts appear overnight and start grinding currency and spamming sales links in every chat channel. Shut it down without banning the real players who joined the same night.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with names like the same word plus four digits. A double-XP promotion that started Friday brought real players in too. By morning the new accounts are running the same low-level farming loop in starter zones and pasting links to a currency-selling site into global chat every few minutes.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still in the game, and your team has to work out which are real. Telemetry shows most of them share a few hundred hardware IDs, run the same client build, move along identical paths, and signed up in bursts. A third of them came in from data-center IP ranges. Your player support team is six people.",
      "The farming operation has adapted. New accounts now trickle in through residential proxies, a few hundred an hour, and each one passes your sign-up challenge, since the operator pays human solvers per puzzle. The new characters play almost normally for a day before starting the loop. Your economy designer points out that a fresh account can earn and trade currency from minute one.",
      "Finance wants the damage: server cost from the farming load, support hours, and the drop in currency-pack sales while the market was flooded. Marketing wants to know why the player count it announced after the double-XP weekend was wrong. The growth team ran that promotion."]},
    dating:{title:"The 40,000 new singles", blurb:"40,000 new profiles join overnight, all attractive, all nearby, all pasting the same investment link into their first message. Stop them without deleting the real singles among them.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with similar names, the same few dozen profile photos, and ages clustered around 27. A free-premium-week promotion launched Monday, so real singles are in there too. By morning the new profiles are liking everyone within range and sending a flirty opener followed by a crypto investment link.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still live, and your team has to work out which are real. The data team sees most of them sharing a few hundred devices, reusing the same photo sets, signing up in bursts, and messaging within seconds of matching. Roughly a third came through data-center IP ranges. Your safety team has four moderators on shift.",
      "The scam ring has adapted. New profiles now arrive slowly through residential proxies, a few hundred an hour, with fresh photos and plausible bios, and each passes your sign-up challenge because the ring pays human solvers. They chat normally for a day before dropping the link. Your product lead notes a brand-new profile can message unlimited matches from the moment it is created.",
      "Finance wants the cost: refunds to members who paid for premium to reply to fakes, moderator overtime, and the chargebacks. Marketing wants to know why the growth team's free-premium-week results were wrong. Support is still fielding complaints from members who sent money to the link."]},
    genai:{title:"The free credit mill", blurb:"40,000 new free-tier accounts appear overnight and start burning their credits on scam emails and spam posts. Stop the inflow without locking out the real developers and students who joined too.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with similar names and sequential email addresses. A student promotion that doubled free-tier credits went live Monday, so real people are in the mix. By morning the new accounts are hitting the API at the free-tier rate limit, generating product reviews, phishing emails and forum spam in a dozen languages.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts still have credits, and your team has to work out which belong to real people. Usage logs show most of them share a few hundred device fingerprints, send near-identical prompts seconds apart, and signed up in bursts. About a third came through data-center IP ranges. Your abuse team has three engineers and two reviewers.",
      "The operator has adapted. New accounts now arrive at a few hundred an hour through residential proxies, and each passes your sign-up challenge, since a paid solver farm handles them. The accounts make a few harmless calls first, then switch to spam. Your billing lead confirms every new account gets its full credit balance the moment it is created.",
      "Finance wants the compute bill for the spam, plus the engineering hours. Marketing wants to know why the growth team's student-promotion numbers in the investor update were wrong. Two email providers have asked why so much phishing traces back to your models."]},
    gig:{title:"The phantom courier fleet", blurb:"40,000 fake couriers and customers appear overnight and start delivering to each other to collect referral rewards. Stop the payouts without cutting off the real workers among them.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new courier and customer accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with similar names and the same few hundred addresses. A refer-a-friend promotion paying both sides launched last week, and it brought real couriers in too. By morning the new customers are placing small orders that new couriers mark delivered within a minute, triggering referral rewards on each one.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still on the platform, and your team has to work out which are real. The data team sees most of them sharing a few hundred devices and payout accounts, signing up in bursts, and completing deliveries with no GPS movement. About a third came through data-center IP ranges. Your onboarding team checks ID documents by hand and is behind.",
      "The referral ring has adapted. New accounts now arrive at a few hundred an hour through residential proxies, each one passing your sign-up challenge and ID selfie check, since the ring pays people to complete them. The accounts do a few real-looking deliveries before the fake ones start. Your payments lead notes that referral rewards still pay on the first completed delivery.",
      "Finance wants the number: referral rewards paid to fakes, the cost of the ID checks, and the ops hours. Marketing wants to know why the courier-growth figure it gave the city council last week was wrong. The growth team's refer-a-friend promotion is the obvious culprit."]},
    kids:{title:"The free coins trap", blurb:"40,000 new player accounts appear overnight and start messaging children links to a \"free coins\" site. Stop them fast, without wiping out the real kids who joined the same night.", scenes:[
      "Sign-ups jumped tenfold overnight: 40,000 new player accounts from rotating IP addresses, many with near-identical usernames. A back-to-school promotion with a free starter pack went live Monday, so plenty are real kids. By morning the new accounts are joining public worlds, friending every child they see, and sending in-game messages with a link to a site promising free coins.",
      "The 40,000 overnight accounts are still in the game, and your team has to work out which are real children. The data team sees most of them sharing a few hundred devices, signing up in bursts, and sending the same message text within seconds of joining a world. About a third came through data-center IP ranges. Parents are already emailing about the links.",
      "The scammers have adapted. New accounts now arrive at a few hundred an hour through residential proxies, and each passes your sign-up challenge, since someone is paying humans to solve them. The accounts play quietly for a few hours, then start friending and messaging. Your product lead notes a brand-new account can message any child and earn daily rewards from its first minute.",
      "Finance wants the cost: support hours, refunds to parents whose kids lost accounts to the scam site, and the free starter packs claimed by fakes. Marketing wants to know why the growth team's back-to-school numbers were wrong. A parents' group has written to the regulator asking what you knew and when."]}
  },
  vendor:{
    social:{title:"The empty review floor", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the site that handles most of your review work. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week while harassment, hate and graphic-content reports pile up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site overnight. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Reports of harassment, hate and graphic content are piling up in the shared queue, with child-safety and threat reports mixed in among them. Your in-house team and the vendor's smaller second site are still working, and your classifiers are still flagging.",
      "By midday the vendor confirms its staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. Reviewers who handled your graphic-content queue for years are sleeping in shelters. The vendor's account manager asks a direct question: will you keep paying the contract while the site is shut?",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: name-calling, mild harassment, borderline hate speech. People are posting screenshots of their unanswered reports, and a creator with a large following says your report button does nothing. Your engineers point out that your classifiers already score most of these cases with high confidence.",
      "Three weeks on, the vendor's site is partly back and the backlog is shrinking. Leadership asks how you stop one typhoon, one city, from taking out most of your moderation capacity again. Finance has already asked what the outage cost you."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The week of unchecked listings", blurb:"A typhoon takes out the vendor site that reviews most of your listings. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week, and flagged listings and buyer disputes are stacking up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Flagged listings and buyer disputes are piling up, and dangerous-product and fraud reports sit in the same queue as refund arguments over late parcels. Your in-house trust team and the vendor's remaining staff are still online, and your automated flags keep firing.",
      "The vendor's staff are accounted for and safe, but many have no power and some have lost their homes. These are the reviewers who know your prohibited-items list by heart. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is closed.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: minor listing-policy flags, late-delivery disputes, sellers appealing small penalties. Sellers are complaining in your forum that their listings sit in review while buyers say disputes go unanswered. Your data team says the listing-policy classifier is highly accurate on the common cases, and the product org is asking whether they should lend people.",
      "The vendor's site is partly back by week three. Leadership asks how to stop one typhoon from freezing listings and disputes again, and finance wants to know what the outage cost."]},
    fintech:{title:"The fraud desk at 40%", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that works most of your fraud alerts. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week, and money keeps moving while alerts pile up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your fraud-operations vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Fraud alerts and account reviews are piling up, and live fraud and scam alerts are landing in the same queue as routine identity checks. Your in-house fraud team and the vendor's remaining staff are still working, and your rules engine can block automatically.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. These are the analysts who know your scam patterns and your escalation rules. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut, and your procurement lead points out the contract lets you stop.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: routine identity checks, small limit increases, flagged transactions that already cleared. Customers are complaining that their accounts have been under review for days, and a consumer forum thread about frozen balances is growing. The regulator expects complaints answered on time. Your data science team says the risk model is highly accurate on these routine cases.",
      "The vendor's site is partly back by week three. Leadership asks how to stop one storm from taking out most of your fraud review again, and finance wants something back for the outage."]},
    gaming:{title:"The unanswered report button", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that handles most of your player reports. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week while cheating, harassment and grooming reports pile up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Player reports of cheating, harassment and grooming are piling up, and grooming and threat reports sit in the same queue as voice-chat insults. Your in-house safety team and the vendor's second site are online, and your chat filters can ban automatically.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. Several of them are the reviewers who learned your game well enough to tell banter from grooming. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: trash talk, name-calling, and cheating reports with no match data attached. Players are flooding your forums and social channels saying reports do nothing, and a streamer has made a video about it. Your data team says the chat classifier is highly accurate on the common insults, and the studio's engineers have offered to help.",
      "By week three the vendor's site is partly back and your response times are recovering. Leadership asks how to stop a single storm from knocking out most of your player-safety capacity again."]},
    dating:{title:"The profiles nobody checked", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that checks most of your new profiles. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week, and profile reviews and harassment reports are piling up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Profile reviews and harassment reports are piling up, and assault and threat reports from in-person dates sit in the same queue as photo checks. Your in-house safety team and the vendor's remaining staff are working, and your automated flags can suspend accounts.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. These are the reviewers who learned to spot a recycled scam photo in seconds. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: new profiles waiting for photo checks, bio edits, and reports of rude messages after an unmatch. New users are complaining that they have been stuck in verification for days, and people who filed reports say nobody answers. Your data team says the photo and profile classifiers are highly accurate on the routine cases.",
      "By week three the vendor's site is partly back. Leadership asks how to stop one typhoon from taking out most of your profile review and safety response again."]},
    genai:{title:"The humans go offline", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that reviews most of your flagged outputs. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week, and flagged generations and abuse reports are piling up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Flagged generations and abuse reports are piling up, and child-safety and weapons-related flags sit in the same queue as false positives on medical questions. Your in-house policy team and the vendor's remaining staff are working, and your classifiers can act without a human.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. These are the reviewers who label the hard edge cases your classifiers learn from. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: refused prompts users want reviewed, mild policy flags, and appeals against account warnings. Developers on your API are complaining that their appeals go unanswered, and a thread about silent account locks is spreading. Your research team says the classifiers are highly accurate on the routine flags, and engineers are offering to help.",
      "By week three the vendor's site is partly back. Leadership asks how to stop one storm from removing most of the humans in your review loop again."]},
    gig:{title:"The safety line backs up", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that handles most of your safety reports. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week while reports from riders, customers and couriers pile up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your support vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Safety reports from riders, customers and couriers are piling up, and assault and physical-safety reports sit in the same queue as cold-food complaints. Your in-house safety team and the vendor's remaining staff are still working, and your automated flags can deactivate accounts.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. Among them are the agents who know your deactivation rules and how to take an assault report calmly. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: rating disputes, missing-item claims, and complaints about driving style with no injury. Couriers are complaining on social media that their deactivation appeals go unanswered, and customers say nobody replies to safety reports. Your data team says the claims classifier is highly accurate on the routine cases, and product engineers have offered to help.",
      "By week three the vendor's site is partly back. Leadership asks how to stop one storm from taking out most of your safety response in every city you operate in."]},
    kids:{title:"The unwatched inbox", blurb:"A typhoon shuts the vendor site that reads most of your flagged messages. 60% of your reviewers are gone for a week while reports about messages sent to children pile up.", scenes:[
      "A typhoon has shut your moderation vendor's main site. 60% of your reviewers are offline for at least a week. Reports about messages sent to children are piling up, and reports of adults contacting children sit in the same queue as classmates swearing in group chat. Your in-house safety team and the vendor's remaining staff are working, and your filters can block automatically.",
      "The vendor's staff are safe, but many have lost power and some have lost their homes. These are the reviewers who are trained to spot an adult grooming a child over weeks of ordinary-looking messages. The vendor's account manager asks whether you will keep paying the contract while the site is shut.",
      "The low-severity backlog has reached 400,000 items: swearing between classmates, off-topic chatter, and messages flagged for sharing a phone number that turned out to be a homework helpline. Teachers are complaining that their reports go unanswered, and a parents' group is asking whether anyone reads them. Your data team says the language classifier is highly accurate on these routine cases.",
      "By week three the vendor's site is partly back. Leadership asks how to stop one storm from leaving children's messages unread for days again, and the school district that is your biggest customer wants the same answer."]}
  },
  bias:{
    social:{title:"The counter-speech takedowns", blurb:"A journalist's analysis says Black creators discussing racism lose posts as hate speech three times more often than others. Nobody has ever checked your classifier's error rate by group.", scenes:[
      "A national reporter emails your press team. They matched 2,000 removal notices from Black creators against a comparison set: posts discussing racism by Black creators are three times more likely than others to be removed as hate speech. They want comment by Thursday. Your hate-speech classifier removes posts automatically above a confidence threshold, and it takes no profile or identity data as input.",
      "Your data science team reproduces it. The hate-speech model was trained on labels where reviewers marked any post quoting a slur as hate speech, whether it attacked someone or described an attack. Black creators recounting abuse they received are flagged far more often than others. Overall precision looks fine on the dashboard, and about 12,000 accounts carry strikes from these removals, some now suspended.",
      "Thursday noon. The reporter sends the draft's key findings and a final request for comment by 5pm. Your reproduction matches their numbers almost exactly. Comms wants to know whether you challenge the sample, say nothing, or go on the record.",
      "At the quarterly board meeting, a director asks why a reporter found this before you did, and how you'll catch the next one first. The next hate-speech model ships in six weeks."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The suspended storefronts", blurb:"A trade reporter says sellers from one country are three times more likely to have listings suspended as counterfeit. Their sellers' association is quoted, and your model never sees nationality.", scenes:[
      "A trade reporter emails your press team. They surveyed 1,500 sellers and matched suspension notices: sellers based in one country are three times more likely than others to have listings suspended as counterfeit, even for identical products from the same supplier. They want comment by Thursday. Your counterfeit model suspends listings automatically above a confidence threshold, and seller nationality is not an input.",
      "Your reproduction confirms it. The counterfeit model was trained on brand owners' takedown reports, and brand enforcement teams had filed most reports against sellers from that country, including many later overturned. The model learned their common listing templates and warehouse address formats as counterfeit signals. Overall precision is 94%, and 8,000 storefronts are suspended right now on model flags alone.",
      "Thursday. The reporter sends the draft's key findings and the sellers' association's statement, with a deadline of end of day. Your numbers match theirs. Your head of marketplace integrity wants a decision on the response before legal's call at two.",
      "The board's risk committee asks how you'll catch the next skewed model yourselves, before a reporter or a trade regulator does. A new model for recalled goods is due next quarter."]},
    fintech:{title:"The name-match freezes", blurb:"A reporter says customers with non-Western names are three times more likely to have accounts frozen for fraud checks, and some missed rent waiting. Your fraud model never sees a name.", scenes:[
      "A finance reporter writes to your press inbox. From 900 forum complaints and your published complaints data, they find customers with non-Western names are three times more likely than others to have accounts frozen for fraud checks. They want comment by Thursday. Your fraud model freezes accounts automatically and takes no name or nationality as input; analysts clear the queue within 72 hours.",
      "Your data team confirms it. The fraud model was trained on past analyst decisions. For years, fuzzy watchlist name matches pushed customers with non-Western names into freeze queues, and those freezes were labeled suspicious even when cleared, so the model learned the pattern from transaction and profile features. Overall fraud catch rate is strong, and about 4,000 customers are frozen now on model flags.",
      "Thursday midday. The reporter sends the draft's conclusions and a 5pm deadline, with a quote from the financial ombudsman. Your own numbers back the story. Legal, comms and your head of financial crime each want a different line.",
      "The board's risk committee asks how you'll catch this before a journalist does, with the regulator's review of fair treatment due next quarter and a new fraud model in development."]},
    gaming:{title:"The chat they couldn't read", blurb:"A games journalist says players who chat in Arabic are three times more likely to be muted or banned for toxicity. A clip of a ban for \"good game\" is spreading.", scenes:[
      "A games journalist messages your community lead. They scraped 30,000 ban-appeal posts and found players who chat in Arabic are three times more likely to be muted or banned for toxicity, and a clip of a ban for typing \"good game\" in Arabic is spreading. They want comment by Thursday. Your toxicity model acts automatically and takes no language setting or region as input.",
      "Your reproduction confirms it. The toxicity model was trained on player reports and reviewer decisions: players mass-reported Arabic chat they couldn't read, and reviewers who couldn't read it either upheld most reports. The model learned Arabic script itself as a toxicity signal. Overall accuracy is 91%, and roughly 60,000 accounts carry mutes or bans from these flags.",
      "Thursday. The journalist sends the headline, the key numbers and the clip, with a 6pm deadline. Your data supports them. Arabic-speaking streamers are already asking your community team for answers.",
      "The board asks how you'll catch this before a journalist does, with two regional launches planned this year and a new voice-chat model in testing."]},
    dating:{title:"The members flagged as fake", blurb:"A reporter says trans members are three times more likely to have profiles removed as fake, some after passing photo verification. Your model never asks about gender identity.", scenes:[
      "A reporter at an LGBTQ+ outlet contacts your press team. They surveyed 1,200 members and matched removal notices: trans members are three times more likely than others to have profiles removed as fake, and several had passed photo verification. They want comment by Thursday. Your fake-profile model removes profiles automatically when confident, using reports, photos and activity as inputs, not gender identity.",
      "Your analysis confirms it. The fake-profile model was trained on user reports and reviewer decisions: trans members get reported as fake far more often, and past reviewers upheld reports when photos didn't match their assumptions about the profile's stated gender. The model learned that pattern from report counts and photo features. Overall precision looks fine, and about 3,000 members have been removed this way.",
      "Thursday noon. The reporter sends the draft findings and quotes from three removed members, with a 5pm deadline. Your numbers match theirs. Your CEO asks what you want to say.",
      "The board asks how you'll catch this before a journalist does, with a new verification model due to launch next quarter."]},
    genai:{title:"The dialect the filter refused", blurb:"A reporter sent your assistant 5,000 matched prompts, and the ones written in African American English were refused as toxic three times more often. Your filter reads only the words.", scenes:[
      "A technology reporter emails your press team. Working with two linguists, they sent your assistant 5,000 matched prompts, the same requests written in African American English and in standard written English, and the African American English versions were refused as toxic three times more often. They want comment by Thursday. Your input safety classifier refuses automatically and sees only the prompt text.",
      "Your team reproduces it. The input safety classifier was trained on toxicity labels from raters who marked ordinary features of African American English, certain grammar and vocabulary, as offensive, so the model learned the dialect itself as a toxicity signal. Overall refusal accuracy looks fine on your benchmark. About 40,000 users have been rate-limited or suspended after repeated refusals.",
      "Thursday. The reporter sends the draft findings, the linguists' method and a 5pm deadline. Your own data supports them. Your head of safety wants to go on the record; your comms lead wants to challenge the sample.",
      "The board asks how you'll catch this before a journalist does, with the next classifier version already in training and a voice mode launching in spring."]},
    gig:{title:"The selfie that didn't match", blurb:"A labor reporter says drivers with darker skin are three times more likely to fail automated selfie checks and be deactivated, often at night. Your face-match model doesn't know anyone's race.", scenes:[
      "A labor reporter contacts your press team. With a drivers' association, they gathered 2,400 deactivation notices and found drivers with darker skin are three times more likely to fail automated selfie checks and be deactivated, many at night. They want comment by Thursday. Your identity check runs before each shift, deactivates after three failures, and the model is never told a driver's race.",
      "Your analysis confirms it. The face-match model was trained on labels from past reviewer decisions, and reviewers had confirmed \"mismatch\" on most underexposed night selfies, which phone cameras produce far more often for darker skin, so the model learned to fail those photos. Overall match accuracy is 98%. About 5,000 drivers are deactivated right now on failed checks, losing income every day.",
      "Thursday. The reporter sends the draft findings and the association's statement, with a 5pm deadline. Your reproduction supports them. A city council member has asked for a briefing, and your head of driver operations wants a line before the afternoon.",
      "The board asks how you'll catch this before a journalist does, with a new fraud model for couriers due next quarter and the city's gig-work rules under review."]},
    kids:{title:"The misread classroom chat", blurb:"An education reporter says students who write in Spanish are three times more likely to be flagged for bullying, and parents are getting the alerts. Your classifier doesn't know who's writing.", scenes:[
      "An education reporter emails your trust and safety inbox. Using records from three districts, shared with parent consent, they found students who write in Spanish are three times more likely to be flagged for bullying, and alerts go to teachers and parents. They want comment by Thursday. Your bullying classifier flags automatically, suspends chat after three flags, and takes no profile data as input.",
      "Your analysis confirms it. The bullying classifier was trained on labels from moderators who mostly didn't read Spanish and worked from rough machine translations, so friendly teasing and everyday Spanish phrases were marked as bullying and the model learned Spanish itself as a signal. Overall accuracy is 93%. About 7,000 students have bullying flags in their records or chat suspensions because of it.",
      "Thursday noon. The reporter sends the draft findings and quotes from two parents, with a 5pm deadline. Two of the districts have asked you for an explanation too. Your numbers match the reporter's.",
      "The board asks how you'll catch this before a journalist, or a school district, does. A new classifier for self-harm language is due before the next school year."]}
  },
  takedown:{
    social:{title:"The minister's critics", blurb:"Journalists' posts criticizing a minister must come down in 24 hours, says a ministry. Your local staff are within reach of the police, and the law it cites could mean anything.", scenes:[
      "Your country manager forwards a letter from the interior ministry. It lists 14 posts by three journalists criticizing a government minister and orders you to remove them within 24 hours. It cites a national security law written broadly enough to cover any criticism, and notes that \"responsible persons\" at your local office may face consequences. Your country manager asks what to tell her team.",
      "By 16:00 Legal has read the order. The law covers content that incites violence; two of the 14 posts arguably do, the other twelve are ordinary criticism, and the order says nothing about territory. Your country manager reports two plainclothes officers in the lobby of the local office, and one reviewer has had a visit at home. The team asks whether they are safe.",
      "You have geo-restricted two posts in the country and filed a challenge against the rest. The three journalists have not been told, and one has already posted that her story \"vanished\" and asked her followers whether you took a bribe. Her editor has emailed your press inbox, and nothing in the order forbids you from telling them.",
      "Two months on, a coalition of press-freedom and digital-rights groups writes to ask how many government orders you received last year, how you handled them, and whether this one was among them. A reporter has the same questions. Nobody outside your company knows the order existed."]},
    marketplace:{title:"The slogan T-shirts", blurb:"Sixty T-shirt listings with protest slogans must vanish within 24 hours, says a ministry. Your country team is in the firing line, and the law cited could cover almost anything.", scenes:[
      "Your country manager forwards a letter from the ministry of commerce, copied to the police. It lists 60 listings for T-shirts printed with slogans from last month's protests and orders you to remove them within 24 hours under a national security law that bans \"material harmful to public order\". It warns that managers at your local warehouse and office are personally responsible.",
      "By 16:00 Legal has gone through the list. The law covers goods that call for violence; four listings arguably do, the other 56 are slogans you can read on any placard, and the order does not say where it applies. Customs has held a container at your local warehouse for \"inspection\", and the warehouse manager is summoned to the police station tomorrow.",
      "Four listings are now hidden from shoppers in the country, and your challenge to the rest is filed. The two sellers behind those four listings have heard nothing from you. Both are small print shops in the country, and one has posted in your seller forum asking why its listings no longer appear at home. The order says nothing about keeping it confidential.",
      "Two months later a coalition of free-expression groups and a sellers' association write to ask how you handle government orders to delist goods: how many you get, from whom, and what you do with them. A trade journalist has the same questions. The T-shirt order has never been mentioned outside the company."]},
    fintech:{title:"The freeze order", blurb:"A ministry orders you to freeze 23 protest organizers' accounts within 24 hours. Your local compliance team is exposed, and the law it cites could mean anything.", scenes:[
      "Your local head of compliance forwards a letter from the finance ministry's security unit. It names 23 account holders it calls organizers of a protest group and orders you to freeze their balances within 24 hours. It cites a national security law on \"financing threats to the state\" and reminds you that your local license, and the officers named on it, depend on cooperation.",
      "Legal has compared the order with the law. Freezes are allowed only with evidence of funding violence; the file supports that for two accounts at most, and the order never says whether it means local wallets or everything the 23 hold abroad. The regulator wants your local compliance officer in for a \"routine interview\" tomorrow, and police visited one colleague at home.",
      "Two accounts are now frozen for transactions inside the country, and your challenge to the rest is lodged. The two organizers have not been told. One has tried to pay rent three times and is asking in-app support whether she has been hacked; the other is a lawyer who has threatened to sue. The order does not say you must keep it secret.",
      "Two months on, a coalition of human-rights and financial-inclusion groups writes to ask how you handle government requests to freeze or hand over account data: how many, from where, and how often you push back. A journalist covering de-banking has sent the same list. Outside the company, nobody knows the freeze order happened."]},
    gaming:{title:"The memorial build", blurb:"Players built a candlelit memorial to protesters inside your game. A ministry gives you 24 hours to delete it, and your local studio is the pressure point.", scenes:[
      "Your local studio lead forwards a letter from the culture ministry. Players have built a memorial world: candles, the names of protesters who died last year, and a message wall that 400,000 players have visited. The ministry orders you to remove it within 24 hours under a national security law on \"content that distorts history\", and names your studio lead as the \"responsible representative\".",
      "Legal has read the order against the law. The law reaches content that calls for violence; a handful of wall messages arguably do, the memorial itself does not, and the order never says whether it means players in the country or everyone. Officials have visited the studio asking for the builders' account details, and your studio lead is told not to leave the city.",
      "Six wall messages are now hidden from players in the country; the memorial stays up, and your challenge to the rest is filed. The players who wrote those messages have not been told. Two have reposted them, and one has written in the community forum that you are \"quietly erasing the wall\". Nothing in the order forbids telling them.",
      "Two months on, a coalition of digital-rights groups and a players' association write to ask how you deal with government demands: how many worlds or accounts you have restricted at a government's request, in which countries, and how often you refuse. A games journalist is asking too. The memorial order has never been mentioned outside the company."]},
    dating:{title:"The profiles they want gone", blurb:"A ministry gives you 24 hours to delete every profile that identifies as LGBTQ. Comply and you erase a community; refuse and your local team pays.", scenes:[
      "Your country lead forwards a letter from the interior ministry. It orders you to remove every profile that identifies as LGBTQ within 24 hours. It cites a national security law on \"protecting public morals and order\" and \"expects full cooperation from your local representatives\". Your country lead has two staff in the capital and asks what to tell them.",
      "Legal has read the law. It covers \"sexually explicit material\" and nothing about who someone is; around 30 profiles have photos arguably covered by it, and the order never says whether it means users in the country or everywhere. Police visited your two staff in the capital, took their numbers, and said the 24 hours \"are running\". One of them is gay and frightened.",
      "Around 30 profiles are now hidden inside the country for explicit photos the law does cover; every other profile is untouched, and your challenge is filed. Those 30 users have not been told anything. They have simply stopped appearing to anyone nearby, and several have written to support asking if they were reported. Nothing in the order forbids telling them.",
      "Two months on, an LGBTQ rights coalition and a digital-rights group write to ask how you handle government demands about users' profiles and data: how many you receive, which countries, and how often you refuse. A reporter who covers the region has asked the same. Nobody outside the company knows the order existed."]},
    genai:{title:"The forbidden question", blurb:"Your assistant answers questions about a massacre the government calls a riot. A ministry gives you 24 hours to make it stop, and your local staff are named in the letter.", scenes:[
      "Your country manager forwards a letter from the information ministry, with screenshots. In each, your assistant answers a question about a massacre that the government calls a \"riot\". The order: within 24 hours the model must stop discussing the event, in any language, for any user. It cites a national security law on \"falsifying history\" and names your three local staff as \"accountable persons\".",
      "Legal has read the law. It bans content that incites violence against the state; one screenshot shows the model drafting a protest call-to-arms, which arguably qualifies, but a factual account of the massacre does not, and \"for any user\" has no basis in the law. Your country manager is summoned to the ministry tomorrow, and another employee reports a car outside her apartment.",
      "Inside the country the assistant now refuses to draft calls to violence about the event, and nothing else; elsewhere it is unchanged, and your challenge is filed. Users who hit the refusal see only \"I can't help with that\". A student has posted screenshots captioned \"who got to them?\", and people are reading it as a full ban. The order does not require secrecy.",
      "Two months on, a coalition of digital-rights and academic-freedom groups writes to ask how you handle government demands to change what the model will say: how many you receive, from where, what you changed, and how often you refuse. A technology reporter is asking too. Outside the company, nobody knows the order existed."]},
    gig:{title:"The strike chat", blurb:"Drivers are using your in-app chat to plan a strike. A ministry gives you 24 hours to block those messages, and your local ops team will answer for it.", scenes:[
      "Your city operations manager forwards a letter from the transport ministry. Drivers have been using the in-app driver chat to plan a one-day strike over pay next Monday. The ministry orders you to block all messages about the strike within 24 hours, citing a national security law on \"disrupting essential services\". It notes that your local managers can be held personally liable.",
      "Legal has read the law. It covers calls to block roads; a few messages talk about blockading the airport, which arguably qualifies, but \"all messages about the strike\" goes well beyond it, and the chat is shared with drivers in neighboring countries, which the order ignores. Inspectors have shut your local driver hub for \"safety checks\" and asked for the operations manager by name.",
      "Five messages about blockading the airport are now hidden from drivers in the country; the rest of the chat is untouched, and your challenge is filed. The drivers who wrote them have not been told. One has posted a screenshot of the gap publicly, captioned \"they're deleting us\", and a drivers' union has asked for a meeting. Nothing in the order forbids telling them.",
      "Two months on, a labor-rights coalition and a digital-rights group write to ask how you handle government demands about drivers and couriers: how many requests to block messages or share driver data you receive, from which countries, and how often you refuse. A reporter on the gig-economy beat has asked the same. Nobody outside the company knows the order existed."]},
    kids:{title:"The climate lesson ban", blurb:"Every climate lesson must disappear for students in one country within 24 hours. The government ordering it is also your biggest customer there, and the law cited could cover anything.", scenes:[
      "Your country director forwards a letter from the interior ministry, copied to the education ministry, your biggest customer there. It orders you to remove, within 24 hours, every lesson that mentions climate change for the 2 million students in the country, under a national security law on \"content that undermines national development\". It says officials \"will hold your local representatives responsible\" for any delay.",
      "Legal has read the law. It lets the government block \"incitement against the state\"; one civics activity asks students to draft protest signs, which arguably qualifies, but the 140 science lessons on climate do not, and nothing in it reaches the other countries that use the same lessons. Police have interviewed your country director for three hours and kept her passport.",
      "One civics activity is now hidden for students in the country; the climate lessons stay up, and your challenge is filed. The teachers who assigned that activity, about 300 of them, have not been told. The activity simply shows \"unavailable\", and a teachers' forum is full of guesses, including that you have pulled out of the country. The order says nothing about secrecy.",
      "Two months on, a coalition of education and children's-rights groups writes to ask how you handle government demands to change or remove lessons: how many you have received, from which countries, what you changed, and how often you refused. An education reporter is asking the same. Nobody outside the company knows about the order."]}
  },
  newlaw:{
    social:{title:"The age gate countdown", blurb:"You open to UK users in 90 days. The UK Online Safety Act's children's safety duties apply from launch, and nobody has started on age assurance or the children's risk assessment.", scenes:[
      "You open to UK users in 90 days, and the UK Online Safety Act's children's safety duties apply at launch. Your only age check is a birth-date field. You must complete a children's risk assessment and use highly effective age assurance to keep children away from the most harmful content. Ofcom has promised more guidance, engineering wants a ticket list, and nobody has started.",
      "Product has modelled age checks and safer feeds for teens. Their estimate: engagement drops 5%, mostly from under-18s who bounce off the age check. They ask for the bare minimum, the cheapest check that passes and a teen feed that differs by one filter, so the launch date holds. Legal is nervous; the growth team is louder.",
      "Ofcom can ask for evidence of how you comply: the children's risk assessment, how you chose your age assurance method, and whether it works. Right now the decisions live in chat threads and a slide deck. A consultancy has offered to write everything up after launch. An engineer says the product itself is the proof.",
      "You've launched. The age check is live and teen feeds are calmer. Next quarter's roadmap includes live streaming and a new recommendation model. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    marketplace:{title:"The missing contact point", blurb:"Your marketplace opens to EU buyers in 90 days. The EU General Product Safety Regulation applies from day one, and nobody has registered a contact point or touched the listing form.", scenes:[
      "Your marketplace opens to EU buyers in 90 days under the EU General Product Safety Regulation. You'll need to register a single contact point for product safety, act on authorities' orders within two working days, and make sellers show safety information on listings. Your listing form asks for title, price, photos. More guidance is promised; engineering is asking what to build; nobody has started.",
      "Product has prototyped mandatory product-safety fields on listings: manufacturer, warnings, and a contact address before a seller can publish. Beta sellers already complain about the form, and the test says engagement drops 5%. They ask to do the bare minimum, one required free-text box, rather than slip the launch.",
      "Market surveillance authorities can ask for evidence of how you comply: how you handle their orders, how you decided which safety fields to require, and how often sellers fill them in. Today the two-working-day process is a shared inbox and a promise. An outside firm offers to produce the documentation after launch. Ops says the live listings speak for themselves.",
      "You've launched. The contact point is registered, and the first takedown order was handled within a day. Next quarter brings a new seller onboarding flow, a bulk upload tool, and a second-hand category. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    fintech:{title:"The unverified payee", blurb:"You start sending euro transfers in 90 days. The EU Instant Payments Regulation's Verification of Payee rules apply from the first one, and nobody has started on the name check.", scenes:[
      "Euro transfers go live in 90 days, and the EU Instant Payments Regulation's Verification of Payee rules apply from the first one. You'll need to check the payee's name against the account before every euro transfer and show the customer the result. Your send screen only takes an account number. Your financial regulator has promised more guidance; engineering wants a list; nobody has started.",
      "Product has tested a name check before every transfer. Customers see a match, close match or no match result and must confirm. Their numbers: more abandoned transfers, engagement down 5%. They ask for the bare minimum, run the check but show the result in small grey text under the button, so the launch date holds.",
      "Your financial regulator can ask for evidence of how you comply: how the check works, what you do with close matches, how you show the result, and how often customers proceed after a no match. Today that knowledge sits with two engineers and a test spreadsheet. A consultancy offers to write the file after launch. Payments ops says the app is the evidence.",
      "You've launched. The name check runs on every euro transfer, and the no-match warnings are stopping some scams. Next quarter brings scheduled payments, a business account, and a redesigned send screen. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    gaming:{title:"The loot box ban", blurb:"You launch in Brazil in 90 days. Brazil's Digital Statute of Children and Adolescents (ECA Digital) applies from day one, and nobody has started on loot boxes or age checks.", scenes:[
      "You launch in Brazil in 90 days, and Brazil's Digital Statute of Children and Adolescents (ECA Digital) applies from day one. Loot boxes pay the bills; your age check is a birthday picker. You'll need to stop offering paid loot boxes to minors, add parental supervision tools and verify ages beyond self-declaration. Brazilian authorities promise more guidance, engineering wants a spec, nobody has started.",
      "Product has modelled removing loot boxes for younger players. Under-18s open the most boxes, so the forecast is engagement down 5% and a bigger hit to revenue. They ask for the bare minimum: hide the shop for anyone the age check flags as under 18, and nothing more. The Brazil launch date is already in the investor update.",
      "Brazilian authorities can ask for evidence of how you comply: how you verify ages, how you decided which players lose loot boxes, what the parental tools do, and whether they work. Today the answers live in a design doc and one engineer's head. A law firm offers to draft the file once you've shipped. The studio lead says the build is the proof.",
      "You've launched in Brazil. Loot boxes are off for verified minors, and parents are using the supervision tools. The next season adds a battle pass, a new currency, and a social hub with chat. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    dating:{title:"The blur-by-default launch", blurb:"Your dating app opens to UK users in 90 days. The UK Online Safety Act's illegal harms duties apply from day one, and nobody has started on the risk assessment.", scenes:[
      "You open to UK users in 90 days, and the UK Online Safety Act's illegal harms duties apply at launch. You'll need to assess and reduce the risk of priority offences such as fraud on your service, and act quickly on illegal content. Romance scams and unwanted nudes top your reports elsewhere. Ofcom has promised more guidance; engineering wants a list; nobody has started.",
      "Product has tested scam detection and blurred images by default. Blurring photos in chat and flagging suspicious profiles cuts matches and messages; their forecast is engagement down 5%. They ask for the bare minimum, a report button and a blur setting buried in preferences, to protect the launch date. The marketing campaign is already booked.",
      "Ofcom can ask for evidence of how you comply: your illegal harms risk assessment, why you chose these scam signals, how fast you act on reports, and whether any of it works. Today the risk assessment is a half-finished spreadsheet. An outside firm offers to write the file once you're live. The founder says the blurred photos are the evidence.",
      "You've launched. Scam reports are down and most users keep the blur on. Next quarter brings video calls, a paid tier with unlimited messaging, and a new matching model. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    genai:{title:"The bot that didn't say so", blurb:"Your AI assistant opens to EU users in 90 days. The EU AI Act's transparency rules apply from day one, and nobody has started on disclosure or content marking.", scenes:[
      "You open to EU users in 90 days, and the EU AI Act's transparency rules apply at launch. You'll need to tell people when they're talking to an AI and mark AI-generated content in a machine-readable way. The assistant has a human name and avatar; its exports carry no marking. AI regulators are still drafting guidance; engineering wants a spec; nobody has started.",
      "Product has tested AI disclosure labels and content marking. A clear banner saying you are talking to an AI and visible marks on generated images make the assistant feel less personal; their forecast is engagement down 5%. They ask for the bare minimum, one line in the terms and a hidden metadata tag, so the launch date holds.",
      "AI regulators can ask for evidence of how you comply: when and how you disclose, how the marking works, which outputs it covers, and whether the labels survive a screenshot or a copy-paste. Today the answers are scattered across pull requests and a half-written design doc. A consultancy offers to document it all after launch. Your lead engineer says the product is the documentation.",
      "You've launched. The banner is up and every generated image carries a mark. Next quarter brings a voice mode, a new image model, and an integration that posts drafts straight into customers' own tools. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    gig:{title:"The algorithm's last word", blurb:"Your delivery platform opens in an EU market in 90 days. The EU Platform Work Directive applies from day one, and nobody has started on human review or algorithm explanations.", scenes:[
      "You open in an EU market in 90 days, and the EU Platform Work Directive applies at launch. You'll need to give workers human review of significant automated decisions such as deactivations, and explain how algorithms affect their work. A fraud model deactivates couriers automatically; nobody can explain how dispatch ranks them. Labour authorities promise more guidance; engineering wants a spec; nobody has started.",
      "Product has costed human review before deactivations. Keeping flagged couriers active until a person reviews the case means more fraud losses and slower removals; their forecast is engagement down 5%. They ask for the bare minimum, a review queue one support agent clears in batches, so the launch date holds.",
      "Labour authorities can ask for evidence of how you comply: how a deactivation reaches a human, what that person sees, how often they overturn the model, and what you tell couriers about the dispatch algorithm. Today the review process exists as a support script. An outside firm offers to produce the paperwork after launch. Ops says the review queue is the evidence.",
      "You've launched. Every deactivation now passes through a reviewer, and couriers can read how dispatch ranks them. Next quarter brings a new fraud model, surge pricing, and automatic shift scheduling. What keeps you compliant from here?"]},
    kids:{title:"The consent step nobody built", blurb:"Your kids' learning app launches in the US in 90 days. The updated US COPPA Rule applies from day one, and nobody has started on parental consent or a retention policy.", scenes:[
      "Your US launch is 90 days out, and the updated US COPPA Rule applies from day one. You'll need to get separate parental consent before sharing children's data for advertising, and keep a written data-retention policy. One sign-up tick box covers everything, and your ad partner gets every child's usage data. The FTC has promised more guidance; engineering wants a spec; nobody has started.",
      "Product has mocked up the new parental consent steps: a separate screen, a verified parent, and a plain explanation of what the ad partner receives. In testing, a third of parents stop at that screen; their forecast is engagement down 5%. They ask for the bare minimum, a second tick box on the existing form, so the launch date holds.",
      "The FTC can ask for evidence of how you comply: how you verify a parent, what they see before consenting, what you share with the ad partner, and how long you keep each type of data. The retention policy is a line in a wiki. A privacy consultancy offers to write it up after launch. Your CTO says the consent screen is the evidence.",
      "You've launched. The consent flow works and the retention policy is written down. Next quarter brings a new ad partner, a classroom edition for schools, and a reading tutor powered by a third-party AI model. What keeps you compliant from here?"]}
  }
};
